# ============================================================================
# Server Environment Variables
# ============================================================================
# Copy to server/.env and fill in real values. Never commit the filled copy.
#
# Each variable below is marked:
#   [REQUIRED]      — the server will not work without it
#   [REQUIRED-PROD] — enforced only when NODE_ENV=production; boot fails if missing
#   [OPTIONAL]      — has a working default, or the feature no-ops when unset
#
# MINIMUM TO BOOT LOCALLY:
#   DATABASE_URL, JWT_SECRET, JWT_REFRESH_SECRET
#
# ADDITIONALLY REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION (boot fails without these):
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_ID, RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET   → checked by config/payments.ts
#   R2_* or CLOUDINARY_*                   → checked by index.ts startup guard
#
# REQUIRED TO HOST A WORKING STORE — these do NOT fail the boot, so a deploy
# missing them comes up green and then misbehaves at the first real request:
#   NODE_ENV=production        without it, dev-only guards stay active and the
#                              rate limit stays at the 1000/15min dev value
#   FRONTEND_URL, ADMIN_URL    wrong values ⇒ CORS blocks the whole frontend
#   RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET    unset ⇒ every payment webhook rejects, so paid
#                              orders never get confirmed
#   REDIS_URL                  unset ⇒ works, but jobs run inline with no retry
#   SMTP_*                     unset ⇒ no order/shipping/OTP email is ever sent
#
# This file is the complete list. Every name below is read somewhere in
# server/, and every process.env read in server/ appears below — verified by
# inventory on 2026-08-29.
#
# Each variable states WHERE to obtain its value. Provider dashboards change;
# docs/deployment.md is the fuller walkthrough if a path below has moved.
# ============================================================================

# [OPTIONAL] development | production | test. Defaults to development.
# Several safety guards key off this — see the production notes below.
NODE_ENV=development

# [OPTIONAL] Defaults to 4000. Render sets this automatically; leave unset there.
PORT=4000


# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [REQUIRED] Local dev: MySQL. Production: TiDB Serverless (MySQL
# wire-compatible, so the Prisma provider stays "mysql").
# TiDB requires TLS — append its SSL params exactly as TiDB's console gives them.
#
# WHERE: TiDB Cloud console → your cluster → Connect → connection string. Copy it
# verbatim including the TLS parameters; do not hand-edit them.
DATABASE_URL=mysql://root:password@localhost:3306/ecommerce_db

# [OPTIONAL, TEST ONLY] Overrides the test database URL. When unset, the test
# harness derives one from DATABASE_URL by appending `_test` to the database
# name. It must point to a disposable database whose name ends in `_test`;
# never set this on Render or to a production database.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# [OPTIONAL, TEST ONLY] Redis used by tests/services/shared-state.test.ts, which
# exercises the Redis-backed rate limiter, OTP store, and response cache.
# Defaults to redis://localhost:6379 when unset. The suite calls FLUSHDB, so this
# must point at a throwaway instance — never Upstash, never anything shared.
#   Local: redis-server --port 6379 --save '' --appendonly no
TEST_REDIS_URL=


# ── Auth ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [REQUIRED] Both are used unguarded (`process.env.JWT_SECRET!`) — an unset
# value fails at request time, not boot. Use long random strings, not these.
#
# WHERE: generate them yourself, two DIFFERENT values:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating either invalidates every existing session signed with it.
JWT_SECRET=your-super-secret-jwt-key-at-least-64-characters-long-change-this-in-production
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=your-super-secret-refresh-key-at-least-64-characters-long-change-this


# ── Razorpay ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [REQUIRED-PROD] config/payments.ts asserts these two at boot when
# NODE_ENV=production and calls process.exit(1) if either is missing. A deploy
# that silently accepted unsigned payments would be far worse than a crashed one.
#
# WHERE: Razorpay Dashboard → Settings → API Keys → Generate Key. Test keys are
# prefixed rzp_test_, live keys rzp_live_. The secret is shown once, at creation.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=rzp_test_xxxx
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=xxxx

# [REQUIRED] for the payment webhook to verify signatures. The verifier fails
# closed, so while this is unset every payment webhook is rejected and paid
# orders never get confirmed.
#
# WHERE: Razorpay Dashboard → Settings → Webhooks → Add New Webhook. You choose
# this secret when creating the webhook; Razorpay does not generate it.
#   Callback URL:  https://<your-render-service>/api/v1/webhooks/razorpay
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=xxxx

# [OPTIONAL] Dev/test only. Set to "true" to bypass Razorpay signature
# verification locally. Hard-disabled in production regardless of value —
# leave EMPTY. Never set this on a deployed environment.
PAYMENTS_MOCK=


# ── Redis / BullMQ queue ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [OPTIONAL] Production: Upstash Redis. Use the `rediss://` TCP connection
# string — NOT the REST URL, which ioredis cannot speak.
#
# Must be rediss:// (TLS). Upstash refuses plaintext, and since every store falls
# back to memory, a redis:// URL fails silently. The server logs its Redis target
# at startup so you can confirm: "🔴 Redis: <host> (Upstash, TLS)".
# A malformed value disables Redis and logs an error rather than crashing boot.
#
# WHERE: Upstash console → your database → Connect → the TCP/ioredis tab (NOT the
# REST tab). Format: rediss://default:<token>@<host>.upstash.io:6379
# Leave the database's eviction setting DISABLED — it is off by default, and this
# database also holds BullMQ job state, which eviction would silently drop.
#
# When unset the app still runs: every queue producer falls back to doing the
# work inline, so a missing value costs latency, not correctness. Queued work
# (order confirmation emails, invoices, shipping notifications) then loses its
# automatic retries.
#
# Redis also backs three request-path stores, all of which fall back to
# in-process memory when this is unset OR when Redis is unreachable:
#   - rate-limit counters   shared across instances instead of per-instance
#   - password-reset OTPs   survive a restart/spin-down instead of being lost
#   - product/category cache  shared instead of per-instance
#
# TRADE-OFF: setting this makes every API request spend at least one Upstash
# command. On the free tier (500K commands/month) that works out to roughly
# 1,800 page views/day. Unsetting this reverts all of the above to in-process
# memory with no code change. See docs/deployment.md "Upstash command quota".
REDIS_URL=


# ── URLs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [REQUIRED-PROD] These two build the CORS allowlist. Wrong values mean the
# browser blocks every API call from that app, which looks like a broken
# frontend rather than a config error. Defaults suit local dev only.
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000
ADMIN_URL=http://localhost:3001

# [OPTIONAL] The server's own public base URL. Its only reader is the local-disk
# branch of storage.service.ts, which builds absolute /uploads URLs — and
# production can never reach that branch, because the startup guard refuses to
# boot without R2 or Cloudinary. Defaults to http://localhost:$PORT.
# Harmless to set on Render, but nothing there reads it.
SERVER_BASE_URL=http://localhost:4000


# ── Storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# [REQUIRED-PROD] Production requires ONE cloud provider — R2 or Cloudinary.
# index.ts refuses to boot with NODE_ENV=production and neither configured,
# because Render's filesystem is ephemeral: a local-disk fallback would return
# URLs that 404 after the next restart, turning a config typo into silent data
# loss. In dev, leaving both blank falls back to local disk, which is fine.

# Cloudflare R2 — preferred (10GB free, no egress fees). All five needed together;
# partially-filled R2 config falls through to Cloudinary, then to local disk.
#
# WHERE: Cloudflare dashboard → R2.
#   R2_ACCOUNT_ID        R2 Overview (also the hex id in the dashboard URL)
#   R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID     R2 → Manage R2 API Tokens → Create token
#   R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY shown once, at token creation
#   R2_BUCKET_NAME       the bucket you created
#   R2_PUBLIC_URL        bucket → Settings → Public access. Enable it, then use
#                        the r2.dev URL or your custom domain, no trailing slash
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET_NAME=
# Public base URL of the bucket. ALSO needed at BUILD time by apps/web and
# apps/admin for Next.js image optimization — see their .env.local.example.
R2_PUBLIC_URL=

# Cloudinary — fallback, used only if the R2 vars above are unset.
#
# WHERE: Cloudinary console → Dashboard → Account Details / API Keys.
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=


# ── Email ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Provider precedence, mirroring the storage service: Resend → SMTP → mock.
# The mock writes .html files to ./uploads/emails so local dev needs no provider.

# [REQUIRED-PROD] Resend API key. When set, Resend is used and the SMTP_* block
# below is ignored entirely.
#
# WHERE: Resend dashboard → API Keys → Create API Key. Starts with "re_".
RESEND_API_KEY=

# [REQUIRED-PROD] The From address. Resend sends from a verified DOMAIN, not from
# a credential, so this cannot be inferred from the API key the way it used to be
# inferred from SMTP_USER.
#
# WHERE: Resend dashboard → Domains → Add Domain, then add the DKIM/SPF records
# it gives you to your DNS and wait for verification. Until a domain is verified
# you can only send from onboarding@resend.dev, and only to the email address
# that owns the Resend account — fine for a smoke test, useless for customers.
#
# Falls back to SMTP_USER when unset, so an existing SMTP deployment is unaffected.
EMAIL_FROM=orders@yourdomain.com

# ── Email (SMTP fallback) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [OPTIONAL] The email service silently no-ops when these are not fully
# configured, so dev works without them. Order confirmations, shipping updates
# and password-reset OTPs will not be delivered until they are set.
#
# Left blank on purpose. email.service.ts decides "configured" by rejecting a
# known list of placeholder strings, and a half-filled config is the one state
# it cannot catch: set a real SMTP_USER but leave a dummy password and it will
# consider itself configured, then fail on every send. Blank is unambiguous.
#
# WHERE (Gmail): the account needs 2-Step Verification ON, then Google Account →
# Security → App passwords → generate a 16-character password. Your normal
# account password will not work.
#   SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com  SMTP_PORT=587  SMTP_USER=<the gmail address>
# Any other provider works too — these are plain Nodemailer settings.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=


# ── Logistics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# [OPTIONAL] Shared secret for the courier/logistics webhook
# (POST /api/v1/webhooks/logistics). Optional only in the sense that the server
# boots and every other route works without it. The verifier fails closed, so
# while this is unset that endpoint rejects EVERY request with 400 — courier
# status callbacks will not update shipments. Set it once you wire up a courier.
#
# WHERE: you generate it and give it to the courier, who returns it as the
# x-logistics-signature HMAC on each callback:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#   Callback URL:  https://<your-render-service>/api/v1/webhooks/logistics
LOGISTICS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
